{
  "case_id": "2008-08-67",
  "document_hash": "a424ed6dde87ec6cf4694435c79bcb30eaaa00c1ed9d93ee1d592790c647b1b5",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Samuel J. Buffone, Esq., on behalf of the Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2008-08-67",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a424ed6dde87ec6cf4694435c79bcb30eaaa00c1ed9d93ee1d592790c647b1b5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a424ed6dde87-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When a jury's acquittal resolves an issue in a defendant's favor, that determination is final and the government may not seek an inconsistent determination of that issue from a second jury."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2008-08-67",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a424ed6dde87ec6cf4694435c79bcb30eaaa00c1ed9d93ee1d592790c647b1b5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a424ed6dde87-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Can I ask you this about the finality of jury verdicts? Is -- does the Constitution require either Federal or State law to permit the -- a partial verdict?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2008-08-67",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a424ed6dde87ec6cf4694435c79bcb30eaaa00c1ed9d93ee1d592790c647b1b5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a424ed6dde87-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Two separate lines of double jeopardy analysis lead to the conclusion that the government can retry hung counts that occur in a verdict simultaneously with acquittals. The first is the principle that the government may, under the doctrine of continuing jeopardy, try to obtain a verdict when a jury is hung. The basic principle there is that the government is entitled to one full and fair opportunity to convict and that the hung counts, when the jury cannot agree, interrupt and prevent the government from achieving that. Double jeopardy therefore does not bar the government from completing its opportunity to obtain a verdict."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2008-08-67",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a424ed6dde87ec6cf4694435c79bcb30eaaa00c1ed9d93ee1d592790c647b1b5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a424ed6dde87-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, counsel."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-67",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a424ed6dde87ec6cf4694435c79bcb30eaaa00c1ed9d93ee1d592790c647b1b5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a424ed6dde87-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Why not, when considering what the government did on its second chance?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2008-08-67",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a424ed6dde87ec6cf4694435c79bcb30eaaa00c1ed9d93ee1d592790c647b1b5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a424ed6dde87-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "A different question: Suppose you prevail. The hung counts are retried. And the jury hangs again and the jury hangs a second time. Is there any point at which the district court can intervene in the exercise of its own authority and discretion just to dismiss the charges?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2008-08-67",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a424ed6dde87ec6cf4694435c79bcb30eaaa00c1ed9d93ee1d592790c647b1b5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a424ed6dde87-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But one hung jury followed by a second when there has been an acquittal the first time around is not customary."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2008-08-67",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a424ed6dde87ec6cf4694435c79bcb30eaaa00c1ed9d93ee1d592790c647b1b5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a424ed6dde87-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "No, because the -- the logic of the situation here is that in order for collateral estoppel to apply, there needs to be a rational jury verdict. And Ashe versus Swenson tells us that in attempting to decide what the jury rationally resolved, we look at all evidence in the record, not just some."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2008-08-67",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a424ed6dde87ec6cf4694435c79bcb30eaaa00c1ed9d93ee1d592790c647b1b5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a424ed6dde87-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Solicitor General has essentially asked this Court to take a metaphysical view of the Double Jeopardy Clause, but the teachings of this Court from Sealfon through Ashe is that the important protections of the Double Jeopardy Clause as applied to issue preclusion must be approached with reason, with rationality, with a non-hypertechnical view in order to protect the public policies that underlie the Double Jeopardy Clause."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2008-08-67",
      "confidence": 0.0,
